    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im studying for my security+ and im stuck on one thing. if we are already hashing passwords, why do we even need to add a \"salt\" to them? wouldnt the hash alone be enough to keep it secure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While hashing reduces the risk of password reuse attacks, it's not a one-way encryption mechanism. A precomputed table known as a \"rainbow table\" could allow attackers to look up your hashed password and find the original plain text, even if the hash is generated with a salt. Adding a unique salt to the password before hashing prevents attackers from precomputing rainbow tables to look up the salted hashes. This significantly increases the time and computational resources needed to crack the password.",
